-- 💡 idea card on the Characters page
insert into public.conditions as c (key, icon, name, kind, parent, cluster, min_age, aliases, about, helps, academic, sources, traits, academic_min_age, position) values
 ('healthy_anger','🌡️','Healthy anger: firm, not cruel','idea',null,null,6,array['Good temper','Standing up for yourself','الغضب الصحي']::text[],'Anger is not bad. It is a signal that a line has been crossed: someone is unfair, pushing too far, or putting someone in danger. Having no anger at all is not healthy either.

✅ Healthy and needed:
• Feeling anger. Someone who never feels it, or always swallows it, gets walked over. Anger that is bottled up does not disappear; it builds up and can burst out later.
• Being firm. Many people don''t respect a line until it is said clearly, sometimes more than once. "Please stop" said softly three times is often ignored; a firm "Stop. I said no." is understood.
• Raising your voice now and then: to stop a fight, warn of danger, or be heard over someone who keeps talking. A loud "Enough!" once is a boundary, not a bad personality.

⛔ When it stops being healthy:
• Raising your voice becomes the only way you get heard.
• It is aimed at hurting, not at stopping something.
• It happens often, over small things.
• It turns physical. Hitting is never part of setting a boundary.

💡 The strongest firmness is often calm. A low, steady "No. If you do that again, I''ll leave." works better than a scream, because a scream lets the other person make the story about your tone ("you yelled at me!") instead of what they did.

So the goal is not "no anger". It is: feel the anger, then choose the level that fits. Firm words first, a raised voice when needed, never the top of the ladder.','Name the line (''That''s not OK''); say what you want (''Stop''); say what happens next (''or I''ll leave / tell a teacher''). Breathe first. Say it once, firmly, then act. Being kind doesn''t mean being a pushover.','Emotion research sees anger as a signal of a wrong or a blocked goal that prepares us to act (Averill, 1982). Spielberger''s STAXI-2 measures anger-in (suppression), anger-out (attacking) and anger control; both chronic suppression and frequent outbursts are linked to poorer health and relationships. Assertiveness, standing up for yourself while respecting others, is the healthy alternative to both passivity and aggression (Alberti & Emmons). Venting by shouting tends to increase anger rather than release it (Tavris, 1989). Aristotle called the virtue ''good temper'': the mean between irascibility and ''inirascibility'', which he thought foolish and slavish (Nicomachean Ethics IV.5).',array['Averill, J. R. (1982). Anger and Aggression: An Essay on Emotion. Springer-Verlag.','Spielberger, C. D. (1999). STAXI-2: State-Trait Anger Expression Inventory-2. Psychological Assessment Resources.','Alberti, R. E., & Emmons, M. L. (2017). Your Perfect Right: Assertiveness and Equality in Your Life and Relationships (10th ed.). Impact Publishers.','Tavris, C. (1989). Anger: The Misunderstood Emotion (rev. ed.). Simon & Schuster.','Aristotle. Nicomachean Ethics, Book IV ch. 5 and Book II ch. 9 (W. D. Ross, Trans., 1908). Oxford: Clarendon Press.']::text[],array['temper','assertive','boundaries','feelings']::text[],16,170)
on conflict (key) do update set icon=excluded.icon, name=excluded.name, kind=excluded.kind, min_age=excluded.min_age, aliases=excluded.aliases, about=excluded.about, helps=excluded.helps,
  academic=excluded.academic, sources=excluded.sources, traits=excluded.traits, academic_min_age=excluded.academic_min_age, position=excluded.position;
